            <div class="of-ftype__faq">
              <div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">What is the difference between safety film, blast film, and ballistic-resistant film for schools?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Safety film (8–14 mil) holds glass fragments together and slows forced entry — the standard entry-level security upgrade for school entry points and classrooms. Blast-mitigation film is engineered for pressure events — it absorbs blast energy and prevents the glazing from becoming a fragmentation hazard. Ballistic-resistant film systems use multiple layers to resist projectile penetration. We assess your threat profile and specify the appropriate tier for each area of campus.</div></div>
              <div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Does ballistic-resistant or blast film meet GSA and DoD standards for schools?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Yes. 3M Scotchshield Ultra series films meet GSA and Department of Defense blast-hazard glazing standards and are specified for federal buildings, military installations, and high-security institutional facilities including schools. We can provide full compliance documentation for district safety reviews and grant applications.</div></div>
              <div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Where on campus should security film be prioritized?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Priority areas are typically main entry doors and sidelights, ground-floor classroom windows visible from the street, administrative reception glass, gymnasium exterior windows, and any glazing identified in the district's threat assessment. We walk the campus with your facilities team before specifying film grades.</div></div>
            </div>
